2) BREADBOARD WIRING MAP (TEXT‑ONLY)
===========================================================

Assume:
  • Standard solderless breadboard with center gap
  • Left/right power rails for +V and GND
  • U1 = CD4069UBE PDIP‑14 straddling the center gap

Rails:
  TOP RED RAIL  = +V (Solar+ after D1)
  TOP BLUE RAIL = GND (Solar−)
  BOTTOM RED    = optional +V mirror
  BOTTOM BLUE   = optional GND mirror

Placement:

  U1 (CD4069UBE) across center gap:
    Pins 1–7 on left side, pins 8–14 on right side.

Connections:

  1) Solar panel
     • JP_SOL+ → TOP RED RAIL
     • JP_SOL− → TOP BLUE RAIL

  2) Diode + reservoir cap
     • D1 anode → JP_SOL+ row
     • D1 cathode → TOP RED RAIL (VDD)
     • C1 (+) → TOP RED RAIL
     • C1 (−) → TOP BLUE RAIL

  3) Power to U1
     • U1 VDD pin (e.g., pin 14) → TOP RED RAIL
     • U1 GND pin (e.g., pin 7)  → TOP BLUE RAIL

  4) Oscillator (using INV1)
     • Choose one inverter input pin (e.g., pin 1) and its output (pin 2):
       - R1 between pin 2 (output) and TOP RED RAIL
       - C2 between pin 2 (output) and pin 1 (input)
       - Pin 1 also tied to pin 2 via C2 (feedback node)

  5) Output buffer (INV2–4)
     • Take oscillator output node (pin 2) and jumper it to:
       - Input pins of INV2, INV3, INV4 (e.g., pins 3, 5, 9)
     • Tie their outputs together on one row:
       - Outputs (e.g., pins 4, 6, 10) all jumpered to same row
     • From that row, run a wire to JP_TIP (stylus core lead)

  6) Back electrode
     • JP_BACK → TOP BLUE RAIL (GND)

  7) Mechanical
     • JP_TIP wire goes off‑board to metal core inside stylus
     • JP_BACK wire goes off‑board to internal metal plate

